### IV. Orthographic and alphanumeric implementation

System: ACE-09 / EOI-CS-001

Every zoning category receives a stable alphanumeric identifier.

Recommended record structure:

`REGION-ZONE-SUBZONE-RECORD`

Example:

`GEO-NA-UR-R-0001`

| Component | Meaning              |
| --------- | -------------------- |
| GEO       | Geographic record    |
| NA        | North America        |
| UR        | Urban classification |
| R         | Residential          |
| 0001      | Unique record number |

### Orthographic rules

* Use uppercase canonical identifiers.

* Preserve complete terminology in the database.

* Avoid assigning multiple meanings to the same abbreviation.

* Preserve original regulatory terminology.

* Record jurisdiction and ordinance references.

* Keep display names separate from machine-readable identifiers.

### V. Geographic stratification — GIS architecture

Classification: STR-01

The geographic system should use independent layers rather than combining every category into one visual surface.

| Layer | Data content               | Function                       |
| ----- | -------------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| L01   | Continental boundaries     | Regional reference             |
| L02   | Country boundaries         | National identification        |
| L03   | State boundaries           | State-level jurisdiction       |
| L04   | County boundaries          | County-level administration    |
| L05   | Municipal boundaries       | Local government               |
| L06   | Zoning districts           | Legal zoning classification    |
| L07   | Parcel boundaries          | Property identification        |
| L08   | Land-use observations      | Actual or observed use         |
| L09   | Infrastructure             | Transportation and utilities   |
| L10   | Environmental restrictions | Conservation and protection    |
| L11   | Resource inventory         | Natural and economic resources |
| L12   | Evidence and audit         | Documentary verification       |

Critical distinction: Land use describes how land is actually used or planned to be used. Zoning describes the legally applicable regulatory designation. They should be separate GIS attributes.

### VI. Color-coded geometry integrity

Classification: CHR-01

Each geographic polygon should contain:

| Attribute      | Description                       |
| -------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Zone_ID        | Unique zoning identifier          |
| Zone_Name      | Official classification           |
| Zone_Color     | Assigned display color            |
| Zone_Pattern   | Hatch or texture                  |
| Geometry       | Polygon or spatial feature        |
| Jurisdiction   | Governing authority               |
| Ordinance_ID   | Applicable legal reference        |
| Effective_Date | Date designation became effective |
| Verification   | Evidentiary status                |

### Chromatic accessibility requirements

* Colors must remain distinguishable in grayscale.

* Patterns must supplement colors.

* Black and white must remain distinguishable from null and missing data.

* Color must never be the sole identifier.

* Color changes must be version-controlled.

This makes the system usable in printed documents, digital maps, and accessibility-oriented interfaces.

### VII. Epistemic verification — Evidence classification

| Code | Evidence state | Implementation                           |
| ---- | -------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| OBS  | Observed       | Field observation                        |
| REC  | Received       | Supplied geographic record               |
| SRC  | Sourced        | Official government source identified    |
| INF  | Inferred       | Derived classification                   |
| VER  | Verified       | Confirmed against authoritative evidence |
| UNC  | Uncertain      | Insufficient supporting information      |
| DIS  | Disputed       | Conflicting source records               |
| COR  | Corrected      | Documented amendment                     |
| ARC  | Archived       | Preserved historical record              |

### Evidence hierarchy

* Official zoning ordinance.

* Official zoning map.

* Government GIS dataset.

* Recorded parcel documentation.

* Approved planning documents.

* Field observations.

* Third-party geographic datasets.

The hierarchy is contextual: a recorded legal instrument may control over a generalized GIS display, and the controlling source depends on the jurisdiction and legal question.

### VIII. Cryptologic integrity — Zoning record preservation

Classification: CRY-01

Each zoning record should retain:

* Original source document.

* Source URL or document identifier.

* Retrieval timestamp.

* Jurisdiction.

* Original classification.

* Normalized classification.

* Modification history.

* Responsible reviewer.

* Verification status.

* Cryptographic digest.

Recommended technical architecture:

| Component            | Standard                                     |
| -------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| Character encoding   | UTF-8                                        |
| Text normalization   | Unicode NFC                                  |
| Structured records   | JSON                                         |
| Geographic exchange  | GeoJSON                                      |
| Spatial database     | PostGIS                                      |
| Coordinate reference | EPSG:4326 or appropriate local projected CRS |
| Integrity hash       | SHA-256                                      |
| Digital signature    | Ed25519                                      |
| Audit timestamps     | UTC, with local-time display                 |
| Version management   | Immutable revision history                   |

A hash can demonstrate that a record matches a previously recorded digest. It cannot independently prove that the original zoning information was legally correct.

